<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monterey, California</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monterey is a city on Monterey Bay on the central coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1770 as a presidio and mission, it was the capital of Alta California under Spanish and Mexican rule. On 7 July 1846 Commodore John Sloat raised the American flag there, and California's first constitutional convention met at Monterey in 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monterey-california.jpg" alt="Monterey, California" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Catalinaschool · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Catalina_School,_Monterey,_CA_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Monterey lies on the southern edge of Monterey Bay, in Monterey County on the Central Coast. The area was inhabited long before the Spanish by the Rumsen Ohlone, one of seven linguistically distinct Ohlone groups, who lived by hunting, fishing and gathering on the Monterey Peninsula. Sebastián Vizcaíno anchored in the bay on 16 December 1602 and named it Puerto de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterrey">Monterrey</a>, in honour of the Conde de Monterrey, then viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo had earlier called it the Bay of the Pines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Despite these voyages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the Spanish did not make the bay a permanent harbour until the 18th century, since the harbour was too exposed to rough currents and winds. It was known for tall pines used to repair masts. The Spanish founded the Presidio of Monterey and a mission church. In 1777 Monterey became capital of the Province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">Californias</a>, the administrative and military headquarters of Alta California and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> and their only official port of entry, and it stayed capital of the Mexican Department of the Californias after Mexican independence.</p><p>On 24 November 1818 the Argentine corsair Hippolyte Bouchard landed about 7 kilometres from the Presidio, under a letter of marque from the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became independent in 1821 the institutions of Alta California stayed much the same until the 1830s, when the secularisation of the missions turned mission pasture lands into private ranchos. In 1834 Agustín V. Zamorano brought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press to Monterey and founded the first print shop in California.</p><p>American forces seized the city in the Battle of Monterey in 1846 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a>, and it remained the capital of the American interim government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> until 1849. In 1849 Colton Hall, built by Walter Colton, hosted the first constitutional convention, where American and Californio delegates drafted California's first constitution in English and Spanish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Monterey had 30,218 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. In the late 19th century the town and its surroundings drew artists and writers, and an art colony formed. John Steinbeck's friends there included the marine biologist Ed Ricketts. The Monterey Jazz Festival began in 1958. Cannery Row and the Monterey Bay Aquarium stand on the north side of the Presidio, and the Defense Language Institute and the Naval Postgraduate School are in the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>'s First Theater, an adobe building begun in 1847, survives and is now closed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monterey, California” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Franciscans under Junípero Serra founded a mission at San Diego that year, and <b>Monterey</b>, founded in 1770, later became the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/alta-california">Alta California</a></li><li><blockquote>On 7 July 1846 Commodore John Sloat raised the American flag at <b>Monterey</b>, and the Stars and Stripes replaced the Bear Flag at Sonoma on 9 July.</blockquote><a href="/state/california-republic">California Republic</a></li></ul></section>
